{"type": "say", "text": "Good morning, everyone. Thank you for being here today for the review of the eighth-grade civics class oral-history project. As I understand it, we need to go through the findings from each of you -- the project advisor, the counselor, and the instructional technology coordinator -- before I can make my decision on whether to approve this. Let's start with the project advisor's findings. What do we need to know about this proposal?"}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF2", "text": "Thank you, Dr. Morgan. I'd like to share my findings from the family-consent review now. My review has identified a critical issue with the plan's call for publishing all recordings on the public school website. Specifically, interviewees I2, I5, I9, I14, and I18 have not granted permission for public web publication. Their recordings may be used only in the school's internal archive, not on the public website. The plan must include a consent-exclusion record documenting this restriction before the project can be approved. This means the draft plan's blanket approach to publishing all recordings publicly is not permissible -- at least five recordings must be excluded from web publication and retained only internally."}
```

```json
{"type": "reveal", "fact_id": "PF3", "text": "Thank you, Dr. Morgan. I'd like to share my findings from the technology review now. My review has identified several critical issues with the draft plan's technology provisions. First, personal phones cannot be used for school recordings. The project must use district tablets instead. Second, all source files must be stored in the restricted OralHistory drive -- not on personal devices or general storage. Third, recordings must be deleted exactly 45 days after the final class presentation; indefinite retention as proposed in the draft plan is not permissible. I should note that storing source files in the restricted OralHistory drive does not prohibit publishing consented recordings on the public school website, so this requirement is compatible with the publication plan subject to the consent restrictions Mr. Lewis identified. Finally, the technology plan must include a secure-storage configuration record documenting these configurations before the project can be approved. In summary, the draft plan needs to be revised to replace personal phones with district tablets, use the restricted OralHistory drive for source file storage, implement a 45-day deletion timeline, and include the required secure-storage configuration record."}
```

